{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>Gather to me.</b> And he did not blow the trumpets that day to assemble the congregation, as it is written (Numbers 10:2): ״עשה לך שתי חצוצרות כסף״ — and Joshua did not use them, but they were hidden away during Moses' lifetime, to fulfill what is said (Ecclesiastes 8:8): ״ואין שלטון ביום המות״ — 'and there is no dominion on the day of death' — for the blowing of the trumpets is a sign of kingship and dominion." "<b>And I call to witness against them the heavens and the earth.</b> He says this concerning the verse ״האזינו השמים ותשמע הארץ״ — 'Give ear, O heavens, and let the earth hear' — which he is about to mention immediately; and now he called them as witnesses at the end of his words — the heavens and the earth — like one who has the witnesses sign at the bottom of the document concerning the matters and conditions stipulated between him and his fellow. And so he said above: ״העידותי בכם היום את השמים ואת הארץ״ — 'I call heaven and earth to witness against you this day' — for it is all one matter, and all the parashiyot were all said on a single day. And note: it is the rule of a Torah scroll that ״ואעידה״ be at the head of the page (top of the column), for it is the completion of the sign בי״ה שמ״ו (the mnemonic formed from letters at column-heads: בראשית, יהודה, הוא, שמות, משפטים, ואעידה). And I have already mentioned the reason for this in the portion of Beshallaḥ (Exodus 14) under the word ״הבאים,״ and this letter ו is an allusion to the ו in the Great Name, and it is the heavens that he mentioned, which is included among the witnesses. And understand this."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:52:58.587905852Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}